obj/arrangement

# obj/api.c is written by the build, and make clean deletes it. Tracked it would make every
# tree that has been cleaned report as modified, and a fresh clone would find it already newer
# than the headers it is derived from and never regenerate it
obj/*.c
libhashcat.so.*
libhashcat.*.dylib
hashcat.dll
include/CL
tools/luks_tests
tools/luks2_tests
.vscode
test_edge*

# tools/test.sh names its working directory test_<unix timestamp> and leaves it in the repository
# root. Anchored and restricted to digits on purpose: a bare test_* would also match
# tools/test_modules/ and hide every new test module from git.
/test_[0-9]*/

# ROCm's compiler writes its scratch tree into the current working directory rather than a temp
# dir, and leaves it behind when a JIT compile is aborted, along with the preprocessed kernel it
# was given. Running hashcat from the repository root is enough to collect them.
comgr-*/
*.hipi

Rust/feeds/*/target
Rust/bridges/*/target

# Left behind by builds from before the bindings moved into OUT_DIR
Rust/hashcat-sys/src/bindings.rs

# Byte-compiled / optimized / DLL files
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class

# Generated docs are overwritten anyway
/docs/hashcat-example_hashes.md
/docs/hashcat-help.md

